WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.080
- [Naz] You play a grown up Dan Torrance,

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:03.550
heavily influenced by his father,

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.060
so my first question to you is

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.750
what did you take from Danny
Lloyd and Jack Nicholson's

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:09.800
performances for your role?

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.940
- I mean I took a lot more
from Jack really because

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.894
I think when you're playing
the adult version of

00:00:15.894 --> 00:00:18.380
a young child like that
there's only so much

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.530
you can get from their performance

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:23.400
and of course he was so brilliant

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.530
but he's like a blank canvas, wasn't he?

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.740
And Kubrick was able to use
his performance beautifully

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.740
but it was very sort of withheld
and I so I got much more

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.260
by watching Jack and I felt very much that

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.550
I know through experience
that we can't help but have

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:45.270
a lot of our fathers in
us as men so I listen

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:46.780
to a lot of Jack on set,

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:50.100
I listen to his interviews with him

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.440
or just to listen to
the cadence of his voice

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.150
'cause I feel we all sound like our dad.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.530
Like if you met my dad now you would know,

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.360
you'd recognize my voice from his.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.193
- [Naz] That you came from him.

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:04.020
- So I wanted to try and do
that with this so I tried to

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:07.480
sound a little bit like Jack
or at least have a flavor of

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.040
him in my voice when I'm playing Danny.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.620
And then that sort of were,

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.060
and he's my dad so I just was
thinking of Jack Nicholson

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:21.203
being my dad and how cool
and strange that would be.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.310
Then I left it because I feel
like that the homages and

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.600
the clever, the satisfying
elements I hope for the audience

00:01:33.061 --> 00:01:36.050
that Mike Flanagan sort of
put into the movie where we

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:40.600
recognize scenes or moments
from "The Shining" in our film

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.930
it's best for me not to really
be thinking about because

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.430
I'm on the inside of it
and not that would be like

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:46.300
thinking on the outside.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.390
- Right, did you talk to Jack Nicholson?

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.490
- I didn't, I must've met,
I know I seen him somewhere

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.970
and you know at awards or
something when I started off.

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.250
I know I've been in his company somewhere

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:01.210
but I've never met him or
spoken to him which is a shame.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:02.870
- Well I thought you
did 'cause you nailed it

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:06.200
but I'm curious what is the
scariest part of "The Shining"

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.693
for you 'cause I know
you watched it as a teen

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:09.120
so it's so scary.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.330
- I didn't really watch
it when it came out

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:13.680
'cause I just knew it had the reputation

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.020
of being the most
terrifying film ever made

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.690
so I was too sensitive to
watch that kind of thing

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.560
when I was a kid so I ended
up watching it in my twenties

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.000
probably for the first
time and I watched it alone

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.830
'cause I thought like well
if you're gonna do it,

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:28.980
you should do it properly,
I watched it at night alone.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.980
And I think it's funny when
he meets and I can never

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:35.843
remember if it's Lloyd
or Floyd the bar man.

00:02:35.843 --> 00:02:36.676
- [Naz] Lloyd.

00:02:36.676 --> 00:02:38.090
- Lloyd, it's when he meets Lloyd.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:39.778
Floyd wouldn't be so frightening would it?

00:02:39.778 --> 00:02:42.240
- Floyd the bar, Floyd isn't frightening.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:43.890
- Scary, Lloyd.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.890
When he meets Lloyd in the
bar I think that's just when

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.030
suddenly he's there and Jack
has that conversation with him

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.300
that's probably,

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.640
that's when I started
hiding behind the sofa.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.260
- Mike I'm so excited to
talk to you about this film.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.970
I mean first off my thought
is how did you pull this off?

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:04.380
You had the unique job of making
a sequel to Kubrick Shining

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.200
and to King Shining so how
did you walk that tight rope?

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.230
With nothing but kind of
intense anxiety really

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:13.590
and I don't know if we pulled it off.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.540
I won't know if we really pulled it off

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.410
until it's out there in the world.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:22.220
But yeah I mean you're kind
of zeroing in on what has felt

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.000
like this intense ulcer
that's been eating away at me

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.463
for the last two years.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:32.640
Yeah I grew up worshiping Stephen King.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:36.130
I saw "The Shining" when
I was very, very young.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:37.600
Way too young to see "The Shining".

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.840
And I've never had a film that
was kind of more formative

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.840
and influential on me and
I've never had another author

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:52.150
who I've idolized this much
so I don't know kind of how

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.820
we went about pulling it
all together short of it was

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.080
a thousand questions every day
that kind of just felt like

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.650
we were always on a tightrope
trying to kind of pull those

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:05.830
two sources together.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:08.080
- Well congratulations
what an accomplishment

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:10.280
and I'm sure it was easier
'cause you are such a super fan

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:11.113
but now I'm curious,

00:04:11.113 --> 00:04:13.300
what is the scariest part
of "The Shining" for you?

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.205
- Of the film or the book?

00:04:15.205 --> 00:04:16.320
- [Naz] The film.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.706
- The scariest scene of the movie for me,

00:04:18.706 --> 00:04:21.830
it's the Grady twins the first time.

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:23.970
Not the first time you see
them which is in the game room

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.320
but the first time he
sees them after the hotel

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:28.380
is abandoned when he
comes around the corner

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:29.850
on the big wheel.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:33.410
That knocked me out when I was a kid.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:36.160
The image of them, their stillness,

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.690
the symmetry of it was terrifying.

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:43.340
I think as I've gotten older
the scariest scene of the film

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:47.180
to me is the conversation
in the bathroom between

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.240
Jack Torrance and Delbert Grady.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.964
- [Naz] So terrifying.

00:04:50.964 --> 00:04:55.950
- Yeah the insidious kind of
casual suggestion of the scene

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:59.050
to me is just chilling.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:00.781
And you killed it recreating it so good.

00:05:00.781 --> 00:05:04.477
I have to ask because
our fans are huge fans of

00:05:04.477 --> 00:05:05.870
"Haunting of Hill House".

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:07.800
I wanted to ask you about
"Blind Man" or what can you tell

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.360
me about it and how will it be different

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.600
from the first series?

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:14.200
Well you know it's all
based on the writing of

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:17.050
Henry James this time
which is really cool and--

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:17.883
- [Naz] The Turn of the Screw.

00:05:17.883 --> 00:05:20.620
- The Turn of the Screw yes,
which has been adapted actually

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:24.990
so many times that it's
kind of impossible to find

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.270
a straight adaptation that
hasn't already been done

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.390
and done perfectly I think in the case of

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:31.360
Jack Clayton's "The Innocence".

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.350
You know so the really
cool surprise for everybody

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:38.010
with this season I think
is that we're not limited

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:40.080
just to that source.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.420
We have access to all of
Henry James's ghost stories

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:47.010
that haven't been adapted to death

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.080
and so it really is kind of turning

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.130
into a very cool greatest
hits album of all of the

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:55.040
Henry James ghost stories
and "Turn of the Screw"

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:57.440
is just kind of the most famous of them.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.730
There's a bunch of other
really wonderful material

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.962
that's all kind of being
braided together for this

00:06:01.962 --> 00:06:02.795
that I think is really cool.

00:06:02.795 --> 00:06:04.190
- Well I can't wait to
see what you do with that.

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:05.940
I love your aesthetic so much.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.050
Especially in this movie
too and I feel like color

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:10.480
is very important to you
so how do you develop

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.470
your aesthetic and what
does the color blue mean

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:14.210
in this film 'cause it's everywhere.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:17.720
- The cool blues have been only increasing

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.970
kind of in my work over
the last few years.

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:22.320
I just love, I love the
way it looks is really,

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:25.620
the not nearly as intellectual answers

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.850
that you want I'm sure
but color is critical.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:31.910
I think Stanley Kubrick
understood that in a way

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.163
that was so far ahead of his time.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:38.750
For me and for Michael
Fimognari who's been my DP now

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:42.980
since "Oculus" kind of
since from the beginning,

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:46.910
we're always trying to find
the palette that not only kind

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:51.330
of starts to connect that
connects kind of all of the work

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.250
we do together into the same family.

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:59.150
But that is also kind of
pulling away the warmth from the

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:02.610
story visually and a story
like this in particular,

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:05.430
when we used colors on the
warm side of the spectrum,

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:07.880
when we did get into our
reds and our oranges,

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:11.180
I wanted it to land with a
lot of impact and that meant

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:13.540
that when we're out in
the world before that,

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:16.340
pulling that warmth out
letting everything get kind of

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:20.649
cobalt get really kind of
cold I thought that could be

00:07:20.649 --> 00:07:23.260
a really wonderful way to approach it.

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:25.610
- Congratulations on the film, Rebecca.

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:28.570
I was so excited to see you
in it and when I was reading

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:31.770
about the fact that you hate scary stuff

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:33.370
and then thought about the role

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:35.930
that you played in this film, I was like,

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:36.763
what was the biggest challenge for you?

00:07:36.763 --> 00:07:38.350
- Cognitive therapy.

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:39.900
That's what it is, you know.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:41.130
Challenge your fear.

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:45.090
It is very different, I was
gonna say, it's very different

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:47.250
shooting something and being
a part of something than

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:51.580
watching it because I am in
utter control of what I'm doing

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:53.560
and I know the outcome.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:56.380
The idea of sitting in a
cinema and things jump out

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:57.640
is just not fun.

00:07:57.640 --> 00:07:59.060
I just get really
nervous talking about it.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:01.000
I hate it so much.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:01.833
- Well here's the thing.

00:08:01.833 --> 00:08:04.510
There's this scene, there's
a murder scene in this film

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:09.020
that is the most traumatizing
that another actor plays

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:11.600
and I'm sure that was, I
mean what was that like?

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:13.430
- Pretty intense.

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:14.540
Unexpected.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:19.540
I didn't think I would have
such a acute reaction to the

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:23.560
acting to the point that I
actually needed to shape up

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:25.360
because I would've ruined the scene.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:31.320
And then I broke down a little
bit after and turned around

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:35.090
and script supervisor
was in floods of tears.

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:39.410
One of the actors of my
clan, it doesn't matter who,

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:42.250
had a memory from something
that had happened.

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:44.210
So that person walked off.

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:46.470
I mean it was, it was crazy.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:47.770
And then we all had pizza.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:50.010
That's kind of how it works.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:50.950
- Typical Friday night.

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:51.800
- And then we have pizza.

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:56.610
But the person who was
creating this emotion sat up

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:57.867
when it was done and literally went,

00:08:57.867 --> 00:09:00.410
"Can I have something to drink
thanks I'm great thanks."

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:02.000
We were going "What?"

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.240
- Such an incredible
performance for me that was the

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:06.810
scariest part of the film but
I'm curious to know what is

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:10.283
the scariest part of "The
Shining" the film for you?

00:09:13.685 --> 00:09:16.910
- Oh gosh, yeah but there so many bits

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:18.310
and I always wanna be smart.

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:25.730
I get very affected by the
sound effects of things.

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:28.730
Obviously when things jump out
but that's the simple answer.

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:34.810
For me I find the cycling, the tricycle,

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.760
of him going down the corridor
and we know what's to expect

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.770
and we don't want him to
stop by the bloody door

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:42.270
and yet he does it.

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:44.470
But it's the carpet to the wood.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.640
- Yes it's like a heartbeat that goes

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:48.040
with a heartbeat soundtrack.

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:51.860
- And you know that Mike,
actually as the geek he is,

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:55.410
he built the, they built
a grown up version of it

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:57.120
and we all got to do it.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:59.550
We literally queued up and we
did the whole cycle bicycle

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:00.404
on the carpet.

00:10:00.404 --> 00:10:01.850
- I read about that he made a big wheel.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:02.960
What was that like riding around?

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:04.040
- Incredible.

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:06.690
And then the competition
was on who did the quickest.

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:09.060
Obviously.

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:09.893
- You?

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.033
- Yes sure.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:15.890
No, I mean Mike.

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:17.840
With pizza in one hand.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:19.700
- That's so funny.

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:23.590
Our fans over at Rotten
Tomatoes are huge fans of "Dune"

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:25.063
and I know you just wrapped on that film

00:10:25.063 --> 00:10:27.960
and I was hoping you could
tell me about your role.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:32.960
- I play the mother, the
protector, the Bene Gesserit.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.890
It's an incredible role.

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:42.860
What is wonderful is Denis
Villeneuve he is together with

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:45.330
the writers creating something
that works today because the

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:48.620
book that was written by
Frank Herbert back in the day

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:52.240
wasn't as metooish as we would've hoped.

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:53.563
We have learned better.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:57.770
So I'm kind of a part of a
change for this character.

00:10:57.770 --> 00:10:58.870
An empowerment of her.

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:01.630
But yes it was lots to learn

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:05.900
and incredible actors
to work with, action.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:08.570
- Your chemistry with Ewan
McGregor is so good in the movie

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:10.710
so I wanna know what was
it like working with him

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:13.080
and I wanna know about the
first time you guys met.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.250
- Well the first time we met was actually

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:17.453
in the reading room in audition.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:19.760
I was extremely nervous.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:21.160
I was twitching all over the place.

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:24.390
I actually told him when we
were shooting like a true knot

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:26.510
scene I told him that I was
twitching all over the place.

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:28.893
I don't know if he saw it
but my leg was doing this.

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.670
That was the first time we
met and I think it was kind of

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:37.020
funny I was acting weird, twitching.

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:38.620
But we had a lot of fun reading.

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:42.380
I think because we grew a
friendship off set we were able to

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:45.430
connect Abra and Dan better
and I'm really happy I got to

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:46.263
work with him.

00:11:46.263 --> 00:11:47.460
He's such an amazing person.

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.480
- What was he like working
with on set when you guys

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:50.313
were in character?

00:11:50.313 --> 00:11:52.480
- Fascinating, I mean it's
so cool just to watch him

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:55.030
switch from Mr. Ewan to Dan yeah.

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:56.960
- What were like the big key differences?

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:58.920
- Well one, the accent.

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:01.310
Two, Dan's more dark than Mr. Ewan

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:03.240
and I thought that was kind of cool.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:05.290
That was wacky but it was cool.

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:08.150
- I like that he, you know
this was your first movie and

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:10.190
Ewan's an actor that's
been around for a while

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:13.480
and Abra has Uncle Dan as a
mentor so I felt like that

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:15.310
there was some sort of similar dynamic.

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:19.470
- Oh yeah I see Mr. Ewan
a lot as my mentoree

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:22.930
and I think that's how we
relate to Dan and Abra.

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:24.910
- Nice, well you killed it.
- Thank you.

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:27.440
- I read that your Mom took
you to see "The Shining".

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:30.020
- Yes, my Mom and Dad.

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:32.347
- So what was that like and
what is the scariest part of

00:12:32.347 --> 00:12:33.480
"The Shining" for you?

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:35.310
- Well, the spirits.

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:36.690
The spirits are horrible.

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:40.410
And also the title cards
I found that so funny.

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:42.020
I was in the movie theater.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:43.550
The sound is surrounding you.

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:45.650
You're getting weird vibes and then bam!

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:47.160
There's Tuesday!

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:48.170
That scared me.

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:50.030
I don't know why but it did.

00:12:50.030 --> 00:12:52.570
- That's so funny that's
interesting though a title card.

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:55.200
What was the scariest
part of filming on set?

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:58.030
'Cause you do some real
serious stuff in this film.

00:12:58.030 --> 00:13:00.460
- It was mostly fun.

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:03.290
But I think the scariest
ones were with the

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:04.713
spirits in The Overlook.

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:09.140
With Miss Massey when she
had all her teeth done

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:11.193
and her full suit,

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:13.990
I really wanted to get sick.

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:15.133
It was gross.

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:18.593
But as a peace treaty,

00:13:19.823 --> 00:13:21.230
I won't scare you if you don't scare me.

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:25.983
We did the macarena to kind of
just smother my extreme fear.

00:13:25.983 --> 00:13:26.816
- What?

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:29.250
Like in costume?

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:33.440
- Yeah I walked in during
rehearsal and I went "Aaaah!"

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:36.030
So in between takes we did the macarena.

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:37.320
- To like not freak you out?

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:38.153
- Yes.

00:13:38.153 --> 00:13:39.160
- Who's idea was that?

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:39.993
Was that Mike's idea.

00:13:39.993 --> 00:13:40.840
- Mine.

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:42.090
- Oh that's really cool.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:43.770
What was it like working
with Mike Flanagan?

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:44.830
He's amazing.

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:47.150
The world does not deserve Mike Flanagan.

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:49.490
He's absolutely, he's so talented

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:52.230
and he gave us so much creative freedom.

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:54.340
I'm so grateful for that
because not a lot of directors

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:56.810
give you that and he actually
called me and Mr. Ewan

00:13:56.810 --> 00:13:58.410
in for a meeting about our characters

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:00.350
and we talked about them
and he wanted to know

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:03.260
if we wanted to add anything
which was super sweet.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:04.093
- And what did you say?

00:14:04.093 --> 00:14:05.133
Did you add anything Abra?

00:14:05.133 --> 00:14:07.260
- I really just asked him
about Abra's character

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:09.075
and what he kind of saw for her.

00:14:09.075 --> 00:14:11.360
(sinister music)

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:13.113
- [Man] But the things that lived there.

00:14:15.704 --> 00:14:18.644
- [Both Twins] Hello Danny.

00:14:18.644 --> 00:14:20.356
- [Man] They come back.

00:14:20.356 --> 00:14:23.106
(sinister music)

